      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here: the sine curve, with x in radians, and a tangent at the origin. Here's where that curve lives. A Ferris wheel turns steadily, and we measure heights in units of its radius. Start a seat level with the hub. After the wheel turns x radians, the seat's height above the hub is sine x. Can we read how fast it's climbing straight from the graph? Draw the line y equals x. The tangent lies right along it, so the slope there looks like exactly one. Now look at the peak. The seat is at the top, height one, but the curve is flat. So the gradient isn't the same as the height. What function gives the gradient of sine at every x?"
    },
